00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.970
So why are we discussing affordable housing again?

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:13.170
It was

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:20.130
six or seven years ago. I worked for the town of Andover. We did a 2,000 in 2015

00:07:20.645 --> 00:07:22.645
incentive housing zone study,

00:07:23.045 --> 00:07:25.765
which then was the flavor of the time

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:32.325
for the state to how to create affordable housing. We did that study. Got, I believe it was $20,000

00:07:32.325 --> 00:07:38.760
from the state to do that study, looked at various areas around town and looked at how those areas could be developed.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:44.520
Lot of good information in that study and certainly a lot of it will be incorporated into this study.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:50.520
But we incorporated a housing zone regulation, so there's an actual regulation in the POCD.

00:07:51.345 --> 00:08:02.385
Seven years later, it hasn't been utilized even at the peak of the multifamily market. So we will dig into as we progress is why isn't that happening? If you provide the zoning for it,

00:08:02.705 --> 00:08:09.400
the thinking was, okay, if you provide zones, then it'll happen. Well, it didn't happen even at the height

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.120
of the market. So what was the problem?

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:15.320
So in 2017,

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:18.280
the state decided they were going to change their

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:21.560
statutes and they created another statute eight-30J.

00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:23.355
Eight-30J

00:08:23.355 --> 00:08:27.035
essentially says every five years you have to adopt or amend

00:08:27.755 --> 00:08:29.515
an affordable housing plan.

00:08:29.835 --> 00:08:33.835
So every community in Connecticut has to have an affordable housing plan now.

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.920
The problem with the statute is that's it right there.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:40.680
That is the statute.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:47.720
This is some stuff about, you know, after it's a proud to approve it and put it on the website, but for the most part, that is the meat of the statute.

00:08:48.265 --> 00:08:49.225
It's all it says.

00:08:49.945 --> 00:08:57.705
So there's no, it's not specified what is in what needs to be in the plan or how you do the plan or who adopts the plan.

00:08:57.945 --> 00:09:10.810
Some towns it's it's board of selectmen are doing it, other towns the planning and zoning are doing it, But there's nothing in there that says the Wetlands Agency can't adopt the affordable housing plan. It just says it has to be adopted.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:17.285
And no public hearing is even required. So you don't even have to communicate with the public by statute.

00:09:17.685 --> 00:09:18.965
I don't recommend that,

00:09:19.445 --> 00:09:20.645
but you don't have to.

00:09:21.125 --> 00:09:24.085
>> Bill, when it says that it'll be adopted,

00:09:25.045 --> 00:09:30.245
what does that actually mean? Does that mean somebody's working on it or they have approved the plan?

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.500
>> The

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:33.380
municipality

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:36.660
has to adopt every five years a plan,

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:41.780
>> but that's all it says. Okay, so it doesn't necessarily mean that somebody's gonna actually

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:42.660
execute it?

00:09:43.385 --> 00:09:48.905
>> No, no, just that you have to approve it. You could approve it and put it on a shelf. Okay.

00:09:49.625 --> 00:09:53.225
So the state recognized it as an unfunded mandate. And so

00:09:53.785 --> 00:09:58.345
two years ago and this last year, they created a grant program

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:00.160
of $15,000

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:09.040
grants and I know Andover applied and that's what brought us here. So we're using state money to create this affordable housing plan for Andover.

00:10:11.405 --> 00:10:14.285
As I said, affordable housing is very misunderstood.

00:10:15.085 --> 00:10:17.885
So most people think of affordable housing like this,

00:10:18.845 --> 00:10:21.085
the old fashioned housing projects,

00:10:22.285 --> 00:10:41.865
but we're not talking about Section eight. You'll also hear, as we progress with this and it becomes more public, you will hear people say the town wants to bring in Section eight housing. Section eight housing couldn't be more further from this truth. Section eight housing is, that's not what Section eight housing is. Section eight housing, there's two types. You could have Section eight vouchers,

00:10:42.585 --> 00:10:49.545
which means that anyone any any any very low income person can get a Section eight voucher, and they can use that nationwide.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:57.740
They can go anywhere and they can come to Andover tomorrow if they wanted to live in a rental in Andover with a Section eight voucher.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:08.775
What it does is it says this is what that very low income family can afford and the federal government pays the rest. So it's guaranteed rental income to the landlord.

00:11:08.935 --> 00:11:10.775
And then there's project based

00:11:11.015 --> 00:11:14.135
section eights where they actually put money in and guarantee

00:11:14.535 --> 00:11:26.970
incomes. Those are not going to happen in Andover And even if there was any thought of it, the community would have to support it, the town would have to vote for it. You're not going to see project based Section eight. So Section eight is not

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:29.050
what we're dealing with here.

00:11:30.995 --> 00:11:37.235
It's not state or federal housing. Those are things that were built in late 40s for returning

00:11:37.235 --> 00:11:44.355
military from World War II, all the way up through the 70s for low income people when there was a lot of federal money for housing projects.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:50.630
And those housing projects are still around, and some of the pictures in the bottom show what they are.

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:56.550
A lot of them are being repurposed or torn down. These are the mills in Meriden.

00:11:57.295 --> 00:12:01.855
Those have all been torn down this past year and building new,

00:12:02.495 --> 00:12:03.935
very low income housing.

00:12:06.015 --> 00:12:17.790
I'd be remiss if I didn't talk about eight-thirty gs, which I know we've talked about before. It's in your plan of conservation and development. We talked about it in the incentive housing zone, and when they did that in 1989,

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:32.805
their conclusion was towns are the problem. Towns keep denying everything. So if somebody comes in and they want to even try to build affordable housing, towns aren't denying it. So this was the way the state was going to get over that and take away the power of the towns to deny things.

00:12:33.365 --> 00:12:37.205
Eight-thirty gs says 10% of your housing has to be affordable.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:38.500
And if you

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:48.820
don't have 10% of your housing, it shifts the burden to the town to justify a denial. So you really can only deny it if it's a public health or safety

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:56.645
problem. Somebody's going to get killed. Somebody's going to get sick. You can't deny it based on, well, it doesn't fit with the character of the town.

00:12:57.365 --> 00:13:02.245
It's too dense. You can't come up with all kinds of different things that you would traditionally

00:13:02.645 --> 00:13:13.380
do what towns do. So that made it very difficult to deny an eight-thirty gs project and the vast, vast majority of projects throughout Connecticut that have been proposed

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:18.580
have many have been denied at the local level anyways and the court has overturned

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:19.940
almost 90% of them.

00:13:21.435 --> 00:13:27.355
So essentially in order to doubt which we talked about so that's eight-thirty gs

00:13:27.515 --> 00:13:29.755
in terms of Andover your housing

00:13:30.635 --> 00:13:32.875
your eight-thirty gs your 10%

00:13:32.875 --> 00:13:36.960
you have in Andover in 2020 you have 51

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:40.480
units that are deemed affordable.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:47.885
There are there are government assisted units which means they got some state or federal funding to build them CHFA

00:13:47.885 --> 00:13:49.725
or USDA mortgages

00:13:49.805 --> 00:13:54.525
and so the total in Andover is 51 so you're at 3.8%

00:13:54.525 --> 00:13:58.365
so you do not meet the 10% threshold in eight-thirty

00:13:58.365 --> 00:13:58.685
gs.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:12.290
Some of you might have noticed that it declined and Jed noticed this and he went to Eric to discuss it. It just turns out apparently some of the senior housing that was government assisted when it was built, for some financing reason,

00:14:13.395 --> 00:14:19.315
it went down to 18. We haven't determined what it actually was. It must have been some type of refinance

00:14:20.515 --> 00:14:22.275
on some of the units or something.

00:14:23.555 --> 00:14:25.555
So you've actually declined in affordable.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:30.130
So the question is what is affordable housing?

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:33.970
Affordable housing is housing that is affordable to individuals

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:36.530
and families making

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:38.290
80%

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:40.930
or less of the area median income.

00:14:42.135 --> 00:14:44.295
Can be rental or home ownership.

00:14:44.935 --> 00:14:51.415
A family at that income level cannot spend more than 30% of their income on housing. 30%.

00:14:51.895 --> 00:14:55.255
It's any of you that have applied for a mortgage is a common banking

00:14:55.510 --> 00:15:02.470
criteria that they look at. Mortgage taxes and insurance can't exceed 30% of your gross income.

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:07.270
And condos, apartments, single family could be any type of housing.

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.230
And per state definition, this is the one

00:15:10.905 --> 00:15:19.785
that is really going to be interesting to you is per state definition an affordable unit must be deed restricted for forty years to limit appreciation

00:15:19.945 --> 00:15:28.000
to limit appreciating to CPI so you have to limit the appreciation to the CPI so very often you buy a house and it goes

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:32.800
up much faster than CPI and that's where you build your equity and

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:34.640
make your profit when you sell.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:46.615
State says they have to be limited. The appreciation has to be limited. So is something that makes it very difficult. That's what limits your affordable housing in Andover.

00:15:48.695 --> 00:15:51.495
These are privately developed affordable housing.

00:15:52.295 --> 00:15:56.640
These are modern affordable housing. This one here is the most recent one.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:15:58.720
Was completed

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:00.880
last year or maybe the year before.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:03.520
This is in Essex.

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:04.720
This is

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:12.735
eight-thirty gs housing. They took advantage of eight-thirty gs. So it's 55 units 30% of the units are affordable

00:16:12.815 --> 00:16:13.775
filled

00:16:13.775 --> 00:16:19.455
right up did not produce any school children. Is interesting the 55 units only produced

00:16:19.695 --> 00:16:22.735
one school child and the child was already in the school district.

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:26.330
Some of these other ones, this is Farmington,

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:28.330
this is Old Saybrook,

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.970
and these over here are

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:33.450
Avon and Farmington again.

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:34.410
So those

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:35.610
are what

00:16:35.985 --> 00:16:40.385
privately developed affordable housing look like much more effectively managed

00:16:41.025 --> 00:16:42.785
and they're in the private market.

00:16:43.745 --> 00:16:48.625
Different types of sources of income: Connecticut Department of Housing has grants to build affordable housing.

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:50.240
CHFA,

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:54.880
the Connecticut Housing and Finance Authority has grants. HUD has funding,

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:59.840
has community block grant money. And then the big one is low income tax credits.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:03.360
This Farmington one was low income tax credits.

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:07.245
Those are 60% of median income instead of the 80%.

00:17:08.285 --> 00:17:12.685
Then others take advantage of eight-thirty gs, which allows you to ignore

00:17:12.685 --> 00:17:16.685
the zoning and you could build at a higher density. It allows you to

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:21.580
make your money by building more units than you'd normally be allowed

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:22.780
to build.

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:25.420
Getting into the numbers,

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:31.740
the area median income for the Hartford East Hartford region is what you are in. Not just your Andover

00:17:32.155 --> 00:17:36.315
median income is a little higher, not a lot. But for the region,

00:17:36.395 --> 00:17:36.955
is

00:17:37.435 --> 00:17:40.555
$105,328

00:17:40.715 --> 00:17:43.595
So 80% of that, and they base these numbers.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:48.440
When you look at it, it's not exactly 80. It's based on the number of family sizes.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:49.800
Hudgov,

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:51.240
huduser.gov

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:53.480
publishes these numbers.

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:55.640
So a family of four

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.880
can make up to $79,900

00:18:00.375 --> 00:18:04.295
and qualify for affordable housing. So that's a lot more

00:18:04.535 --> 00:18:09.815
than the very low income that you see in the cities. This is more of working

00:18:10.295 --> 00:18:11.175
families

00:18:12.455 --> 00:18:13.175
with

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:15.530
you know, a landscaper

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:16.410
or

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:19.690
a cafeteria worker in schools

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:21.290
or things like that.

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:23.930
So those are the figures that you have. One

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:26.330
person 55.9,

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:28.010
two sixty three,

00:18:28.705 --> 00:18:33.425
three point seven one and four point seven nine. So those are the 80% limits.

00:18:33.745 --> 00:18:35.745
So we need to build housing

00:18:36.465 --> 00:18:37.825
to people at

00:18:38.225 --> 00:18:41.185
or below that limit to qualify for

00:18:41.825 --> 00:18:42.945
affordable housing.

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:48.470
In terms of the rental, that's a little more difficult because

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:51.350
they use for the rental the

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:54.310
fair market rent as determined by HUD.

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:56.870
So the fair market rent for this region

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:58.630
is

00:18:57.975 --> 00:19:00.695
below. It's a one bedroom eight

00:19:00.775 --> 00:19:08.215
thirty, two bedroom ten twenty, three bedroom twelve seventy. Not many people are building four bedrooms anymore.

00:19:08.375 --> 00:19:09.895
So ten twenty

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:18.700
that would translate to what would really work is about $2 a square foot. About 500 square feet is about all you could really build

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:23.020
